On the translated API 35 emulator an empty installation entered the
actual title callback, created its 0700 data child and cleanly
reported the missing catalogue. A subsequent launch with 198 locally
provisioned canonical manifests and 416 distinct payload objects
rendered the title and the touch panel, selected OpenSL ES audio and
retained Activity focus. No original media was packaged in the APK.
A fresh-install repeat observed viewport pixel changes after two
Start touches and a held Right touch. Home and foreground restoration
observed native pause/resume, surface recreation and the expected
touch-panel color returning. This is evidence of interactive startup
and presentation, not full gameplay parity or audible playback.

An intermediate attempt to transfer data through ADB exec-in was
incomplete; the byte-counted private archive transfer succeeded.
A later cold restart of the retained installation did not show the
expected panel within the diagnostic window; the reason was not
captured. Fresh installation and provisioning reproduced the passing
interactive result. Repeatable cold-restart acceptance, persistent
settings writes in the actual title, production package automation,
audible audio, physical ARM64, controllers and sensors remain open.
The isolated emulator was shut down.

Retained-installation checks of the preceding diagnostic APK across two
full emulator boots observed three successful process-cold launches out
of four. Successful launches recovered the expected touch panel and
foreground Activity and selected OpenSL ES. The unsuccessful launch
destroyed and recreated the Activity in the same process, emitted an
EGL release diagnostic and returned to the home launcher. The cause
remains unresolved. No reinstall or asset reprovisioning was used for
these checks. The isolated emulator was shut down.

The local package command now builds an unsigned debug ARM64 APK from
locked Rust and SDL2 source with SDK 35 and NDK r28c. The title-specific
launcher uses the existing safe JNI entry. Exact APK admission accepts
the binary manifest, empty AAPT2 resource table, DEX and two aligned
AArch64 libraries; additional archive members are rejected. The
independent package check verified 137 files and 12,270,361 bytes
including dependency licence documents; the APK is 11,637,997 bytes.
AAPT2 identifies API 24 minimum, API 35 target and ARM64-only native
code. SDK zipalign independently verifies 16 KiB alignment. This is
unsigned development packaging, not a signed or portable release.

A separate copy of the command-produced APK was signed with a disposable
local key; SDK v2/v3 signature verification and 16 KiB alignment passed.
A fresh translated API 35 emulator installation succeeded. The empty
installation reported the missing catalogue and created the private
application data directory. After byte-counted local provisioning, the
next launch entered the title, selected OpenSL ES and displayed the
touch panel. Two Start taps and a held directional touch produced
viewport pixel changes. Home and foreground restoration reproduced the
expected panel color with native pause/resume and surface recreation.
The settings directory remained empty. The emulator was shut down.
These checks do not establish audible output, physical ARM64 support,
persistent writes, gameplay parity or repeatable cold-restart behavior.

A retained-installation repeat observed one failed and seven successful
process-cold launches. The failure coincided with a dynamic system UI
resource-overlay update: ActivityTaskManager relaunched the Activity
with configuration mask 80000000. During destruction the old SDL
runner returned and requested finish on the Activity token, which
also retired the replacement. A controlled font-scale change (mask
40000000) reproduced the same relaunch and app-requested finish.

The pinned SDL Java adaptation captures the owning Activity and worker
and posts completion to the UI thread. Only the still-current worker
of a live, non-recreating Activity may clear the worker and request
finish. A separately built diagnostic with the same title libraries
and this adaptation survived three forced font-scale recreations,
rendered the touch panel again and retained normal menu Exit behavior.
The Rust source transformation rejects missing, duplicated and unknown
upstream fragments. Activity recreation restarts the title; gameplay
state restoration across recreation has not been established.

On the translated emulator the actual title settings menu wrote
app-private display, master-volume and effects-volume slots with
0600 permissions. After a process-cold restart their contents remained
byte-identical and the title logged both restored volume levels at 75
percent. The display slot contained fill/windowed. Locale, controller,
physical ARM64, audible playback and production release signing are
not established by these tests. The emulator was shut down.

The repaired workspace packaging command produced an 11,650,421-byte
APK; the independent receipt checker verified 137 files totaling
12,283,355 bytes. A separate locally signed copy passed APK schemes
v2/v3 and SDK 16 KiB alignment. On a fresh private translated-emulator
installation, two forced font-scale recreations both retained the
foreground title and touch panel. Subsequent cold starts, including
across emulator boots, recovered the title after local provisioning.
The actual menu saved Spanish (es-MX), display and both volume slots.
All four files were byte-identical after a cold restart, the Spanish
main-menu pixels were nearly identical, and cycling the restored Fill
mode produced Stretch rather than the initial default. The title
logged restored master and effects gains of 75 percent. A later menu
change to zero master gain survived a cold launch; 75 was restored
before shutdown.

Host PulseAudio initialization failed in the isolated emulator, including
an ALSA-selected attempt. The QEMU WAV backend captured stereo 44.1 kHz
PCM without using a host microphone or speakers. A synthetic Android
tone verified that this route can carry a signal. In one subsequent
actual-title attack trial AudioFlinger identified the title's active
48 kHz stereo OpenSL ES track and the recorded attack interval peaked
at -44.69 dBFS. The guest media volume was 5 of 15. Other trials,
including a paired normal/muted attempt, captured near-silence
(-84.29 dBFS peak) and the virtual backend advanced much more slowly
than elapsed wall time. These results establish an admitted output
track and one non-silent recording, not repeatable audible playback,
reliable volume attenuation, latency or audio quality. Physical ARM64,
physical controller and sensor checks, soundtrack playback and release
signing remain open. The private emulator and diagnostic host audio
sink were stopped; original media and keys remain outside the package.

A subsequent isolated ARM64 JNI diagnostic uses the actual shared
NativeAudio queue and submits 576,000 synthetic stereo frames at
48 kHz. It retains the full paused queue, resumes, observes monotonic
drain to zero, pauses and clears, and returns 42. Two independent
translated API 35 emulator runs reproduce all three four-second
patterns in the 44.1 kHz WAV capture: 440 Hz left only, 880 Hz right
only, 660 Hz left with 330 Hz right, and silence. Representative
steady-state tone amplitudes are 283.5 to 283.7 signed-16 sample units;
silent channels have no corresponding tone. The observed guest media
level is 5 of 15. A requested zero level was not reliably observed
and is not evidence of guest volume attenuation.

The repaired signed title package was then installed in a separate
private AVD with a 320 by 640 display, two cores, 2 GiB RAM and the
QEMU WAV output backend. A canonical opening-street music manifest
and its single 10,429,144-byte payload were admitted after independent
length and generated-object digest checks. The package itself was
unchanged; the existing local base catalogue and the selected music
were provisioned into the app-private asset directory. The launch
reported the actual OpenSL ES output and selected source levels.

At verified guest media level 5 of 15 and master 100 percent, music
100 percent with effects zero produced non-silent gameplay and resumed
gameplay captures (peaks -42.92 and -43.46 dBFS). The menu and pause
intervals were digitally silent. Each gameplay window contained over
526,000 stereo frames at 44.1 kHz. The otherwise equivalent cold
launch with music zero produced digital silence during gameplay,
pause and resumed gameplay. A scan of the full-volume recording
found no gaps of 15 ms or longer below -80 dBFS in the initial
gameplay window, but three gaps totalling 164 ms after resume.

A separate pair of launches isolated combat effects with music zero.
At effects 100 percent, a sequence of 12 native keyboard attacks and
another sequence after pause/resume produced peaks of -38.40 and
-48.23 dBFS. The idle gameplay also contained combat effects, so this
does not establish the exact contribution of each attack. At effects
zero the same input sequences peaked at -84.29 dBFS, near the capture
floor. Both pause intervals were digitally silent. Source levels were
confirmed from the actual title diagnostics, not inferred from the
requested settings alone. The capture used neither a host speaker nor
a microphone.

These paired results establish repeatable translated-emulator PCM
output for the shared audio queue and actual title effects and opening
street music, including independent zero-level controls and pause
silence. They do not establish physical speaker output, exact cue or
soundtrack parity, gap-free playback, latency, high-resolution emulator
audio performance, physical ARM64, controllers, sensors or production
release signing. No original content, diagnostic recordings, or signing
keys are distributed. The emulator was shut down.
